.vercel

# Build output. Vercel runs `npm run build` from source (see vercel.json), so a
# committed bundle is 165 files of noise that go stale the moment anyone edits a
# component — and a stale bundle in a repo is worse than none, because it looks
# authoritative.
dist/

# Retired assets: twenty files that were an Arial <text> node impersonating a
# trademark. Kept on disk so the removal is reversible, kept out of the repo so
# nobody mistakes them for logos.
public/logos/_retirados_arial/

# Orphaned media from abandoned directions — 26 MB that NOTHING references.
# Verified by searching all 235 source files: zero hits for any of them. The hero
# reads /hero/hero-familia-*.{avif,webp,jpg}, which is tracked. These stay on disk
# in case a direction is revived; they do not enter the history, because 26 MB of
# unused video cannot be taken back out of a public repo without rewriting it.
public/landing-rivr-bg.mp4
public/landing-rivr-scene.png
public/uploads/hero-video.mp4
public/hero-city.png
public/hero-cardeep-scene.png
public/hero-road.png
public/hero-family.png
